./iterations/iter_012/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:3:The explorer’s core finite-prefix dual is worth pursuing. I do not see a fatal flaw in the LP/minimax formula under the intended setting: deterministic exogenous known prefix, horizon-oblivious randomized marginal strategy, expected pseudo-regret, and left-limit/infimum treatment of strict crossing.
./iterations/iter_012/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:5:The main critique is scope. The dual is an exact finite-prefix value, not yet a learnability characterization. The unresolved hard point is projective consistency: finite-prefix optimizers may depend on \(n\), so \(D_n(P)=o(n)\) is necessary for anytime sublinear regret but not proved sufficient.
./iterations/iter_012/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:15:- **Plausible but incomplete:** The statement “\(D_n(P)=o(n)\) is necessary” is correct under the expected anytime model, but sufficiency is open. The explorer correctly flags this; it should be treated as the main gap.
./iterations/iter_012/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:23:- **Alternating path:** \(P_{2k-1}=1/2,\ P_{2k}=1\). The explorer’s stress test survives: formal posting \(1^-\) at every time gives \(D_n=0\), while \(\sum_t\delta(C_t)\) is linear. Good evidence that this dual removes CE-018’s false positive.
./iterations/iter_012/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:29:- **Rare branch:** A large pathwise \(D_n\) on a small-probability branch does not imply large expected regret unless multiplied by branch probability. This is the same obstruction as CE-006/CE-011.
./iterations/iter_012/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:31:- **Optimizer consistency stress test:** Look for paths where \(D_n/n\to0\) but finite-prefix near-optimizers oscillate incompatibly. No counterexample found, but this is the right way to attack sufficiency.
./iterations/iter_012/idea_02/critic_01_on_explorer_01.md:35:No conflict found. The finite-prefix dual is basically finite zero-sum game/LP duality, consistent with Sion-style minimax background. Luo-Schapiro’s unknown-horizon minimax work is conceptually relevant to horizon randomization, but it does not prove this path-specific delayed-fill dual. Robust/intertemporal pricing sources remain adjacent.
