./logs/iter_018_ideas.stderr.log:5816:{"bibliography_candidates":[{"authors":"Thibaut Vidal, Patrick Jaillet, Nelson Maculan","relevance":"Exact polynomial algorithms for resource allocation with nested partial-sum constraints; directly relevant to deriving a pooling or decomposition rule for the unequal-low reduced LP.","title":"A Decomposition Algorithm for Nested Resource Allocation Problems","url":"https://doi.org/10.1137/140965119","venue_year":"SIAM Journal on Optimization 26(2):1322-1340, 2016"},{"authors":"Thibaut Vidal, Daniel Gribel, Patrick Jaillet","relevance":"Studies separable convex optimization with nested lower and upper partial-sum constraints, closer to the capacity and low-horizon inequality structure in the proposed drought LP.","title":"Separable Convex Optimization with Nested Lower and Upper Constraints","url":"https://doi.org/10.1287/ijoo.2018.0004","venue_year":"INFORMS Journal on Optimization 1(1):71-90, 2019"},{"authors":"Jan de Leeuw, Kurt Hornik, Patrick Mair","relevance":"Generalized PAVA and active-set methods for isotone optimization; useful proof technology for a possible pooling rule in the unequal-low dual.","title":"Isotone Optimization in R: Pool-Adjacent-Violators Algorithm (PAVA) and Active Set Methods","url":"https://doi.org/10.18637/jss.v032.i05","venue_year":"Journal of Statistical Software 32(5):1-24, 2009"},{"authors":"Ravindra K. Ahuja, James B. Orlin","relevance":"Fast algorithms for separable convex objectives under chain constraints; useful background if the reduced LP can be transformed into a generalized isotonic or chain-constrained problem.","title":"A Fast Scaling Algorithm for Minimizing Separable Convex Functions Subject to Chain Constraints","url":"https://doi.org/10.1287/opre.49.5.784.10601","venue_year":"Operations Research 49(5):784-789, 2001"},{"authors":"Zeyang Wu, Kameng Nip, Qie He","relevance":"Combinatorial algorithms for nested bound resource allocation, including infeasibility-guided divide-and-conquer and submodular-constraint ideas that may inform finite-prefix LP algorithms.","title":"A New Combinatorial Algorithm for Separable Convex Resource Allocation with Nested Bound Constraints","url":"https://doi.org/10.1287/ijoc.2020.1006","venue_year":"INFORMS Journal on Computing 33(3):1197-1212, 2021"}],"ideas":[{"id":"idea_01","risks":"The rounding-to-low-levels dominance may fail at a strict-crossing boundary, the old-high subtraction S_j=V^*_{s_j}-hH may miss a comparator regime, or intermediate zero horizons may create constraints not represented by the low horizons.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","strict-crossing cleanup","finite-prefix LP duality"],"summary":"Prove or disprove the solver's full-to-reduced LP reduction for one unequal-low drought. Start from the accepted finite-prefix D_n primal in the formal left-limit model, partition quote coordinates into protected old-high, useful segment, and final-only classes, justify the action menu L union {h}, derive the low-horizon constraints and final-high debt constraint, and show all intermediate zero horizons are dominated by the latest low constraint.","title":"Audit Unequal-Low LP Reduction","why_promising":"This is the immediate bottleneck in iteration 18. A clean proof promotes the candidate LP to an accepted local theorem; a failure gives a precise counterexample to the solver attempt."},{"id":"idea_02","risks":"The displayed dual may be only the dual of the reduced LP, not of the full finite-prefix LP; dual mass on intermediate horizons could improve the certificate; low-level menus may need running maxima not included in L.","suggested_lenses":["dual certificate","LP verification","counterexample search"],"summary":"Attack the same theorem from the finite-prefix dual. Restrict dual support to low horizons and the final high, compute the per-segment support terms, and verify that they match the solver's psi_i expression. Then ask whether any omitted horizon can increase the full dual objective in one-drought paths.","title":"Dualize The Drought LP","why_promising":"The solver's dual formula is compact and may be the cleanest route to a lower bound. It also directly checks the final-debt term and the S_j normalization."},{"id":"idea_03","risks":"The reduced LP may be a generic piecewise-linear program with no clean pooling law; nonmonotone lows can destroy the chain structure that PAVA relies on; active-set formulas may become more complex than useful.","suggested_lenses":["algorithm design","KKT conditions","nested resource allocation"],"summary":"Assuming the reduced LP is exact, derive a pooling, PAVA-like, or nested-resource-allocation algorithm from its dual. Use complementary slackness to identify active low constraints, active segment levels c, and the beta/alpha tradeoff between low payoff and final high debt. Regression-test on two- and three-low examples, including nonmonotone low sequences.","title":"Pooling Rule For Unequal Lows","why_promising":"A structural solver would turn the local LP into a usable surplus/debt rule and might reveal the right state variable for repeated droughts."},{"id":"idea_04","risks":"Finite experiments may pass without yielding insight; numerical LP tolerances can hide strict-crossing or left-limit issues; exhaustive search grows quickly with path length and alphabet size.","suggested_lenses":["counterexample search","finite LP computation","rational certificates"],"summary":"Build a small exact finite-prefix LP regression suite. For grids of lows such as {0.25,0.5,0.75}, enumerate one-drought paths with small M, N_i, L, compute D_n from LB-031, compute the proposed reduced LP, and compare primal/dual certificates. Prioritize minimal failing examples, then extend to repeated droughts.","title":"Exact LP Regression Suite","why_promising":"This gives a precise independent check of the solver's proposed reduction and can quickly find the first missing constraint or indexing error."},{"id":"idea_05","risks":"Single-drought formulas may not compose; the necessary state may be a high-dimensional vector of level-specific debts; experiments may only show dilution without a theorem.","suggested_lenses":["gluing","surplus-debt recursion","finite-alphabet paths"],"summary":"Study two or more unequal-low droughts sharing future highs. Compare the full finite-prefix value with sums or compositions of the single-drought LP. Track how low-level quote mass creates persistent high debt and whether debt can be summarized by level, by segment, or by a scalar workload.","title":"Repeated-Drought High Debt","why_promising":"The notes identify repeated droughts and cumulative high debt as the main remaining route toward the D_n=o(n) gluing problem. This direction tests whether the new local LP is composable."},{"id":"idea_06","risks":"The augmented certificate may become tautological or too close to the full D_n dual; separated-block examples already show naive boundary charging fails, so a useful localization theorem needs extra structure.","suggested_lenses":["proof strategy","boundary defect","gluing theorem"],"summary":"Define a boundary-augmented prefix LP that keeps late shadow thresholds in the local certificate. Use the one-drought LP as a model: lows are local horizons, the resolving high is a shadow objective creating debt. Explore whether repeated augmented boundary defects either force comparable-scale D_n or are exactly absorbed by later quote opportunities.","title":"Boundary-Augmented Localization","why_promising":"This addresses the known failure of direct dual localization while preserving the information that made separated and drought examples analyzable."}],"solution_status":"unsolved"}
