./logs/iter_015_synthesis.stderr.log:1358:Iteration 14 found no direct external theorem proving gluing or a separator. The internal progress is an exact separated-block value, an exact dual boundary identity, and a finite-alphabet dual reduction. Fulkerson-Gross supports the interval-incidence vocabulary for the finite-alphabet formula, Renault 2014 and existing uniform-value sources reinforce the need for stability beyond scalar finite-prefix values, and pseudonorm/approachability and countably-infinite LP sources are possible formulation tools only.
./logs/iter_015_synthesis.stderr.log:1555:Iteration 12's exact finite-prefix LP passes this stress test: in the formal left-limit model, posting \(1^-\) at every time gives \(D_n(P)=0\) for every finite prefix, because the exact fixed-comparator value is matched rather than upper-bounded by \(\sum_tM_{t,T}\).
./logs/iter_015_synthesis.stderr.log:1561:The finite-prefix horizon-conflict value \(D_n(P)\) is necessary for known-path anytime learnability: one \(o(T)\)-regret policy implies \(D_n(P)=o(n)\). However \(D_n=o(n)\) alone has not been shown sufficient. The possible obstruction is that near-optimal finite-prefix marginal families for increasing \(n\) may be incompatible: an optimizer for a long prefix can sacrifice an older horizon by an amount small relative to \(n\) but large relative to that older horizon. The fixed-envelope compactness criterion avoids this by imposing the same bounds \(e_T=o(T)\) for every finite LP.
./logs/iter_015_synthesis.stderr.log:1563:Future work should either construct a deterministic path with \(D_n=o(n)\) but no projectively consistent \(o(T)\)-regret marginals, or prove a gluing theorem converting small \(D_n\) into a fixed sublinear envelope.
./logs/iter_015_synthesis.stderr.log:1569:The abstract parity construction from iteration 13 shows why scalar normalized finite-prefix values do not imply projective consistency in arbitrary compact feasibility problems. With special horizons \(N_k\) and a single decision \(x\in\{0,1\}\), finite prefixes can always choose the current parity and pay only the previous special-horizon loss \(o(n)\), while every fixed decision fails on infinitely many special horizons.
