./logs/iter_012_idea_02_explorer_01.stdout.log:59:where \(\rho_t(\lambda)\) is the per-time supremum term. Thus if \(D_n(P)\ge \epsilon n\) infinitely often, every horizon-oblivious policy has \(\limsup_T R_T/T\ge \epsilon\).
./logs/iter_012_idea_02_explorer_01.stdout.log:94:A useful sanity check is the alternating path \(1/2,1,1/2,1,\ldots\). The suffix-menu sum is linear, but \(D_n(P)=0\) in the left-limit value: posting \(1^-\) at every time matches the exact best fixed comparator for every finite prefix. This confirms the dual avoids CE-018’s false positive.
./logs/iter_012_idea_02_explorer_01.stdout.log:102:\(D_n(P)=o(n)\) is necessary for sublinear known-path anytime regret, but I do not see a proof that it is sufficient. Finite-prefix optimizers may depend on \(n\) incompatibly.
./logs/iter_012_idea_02_explorer_01.stdout.log:108:The alternating path is the key positive stress test: the old local suffix-menu condition is linear, but the finite-prefix dual value is zero.
./logs/iter_012_idea_02_explorer_01.stdout.log:110:Rare-event hard prefixes remain an obstruction to essential-sup interpretations. A pathwise \(D_n\) certificate on a rare branch must be probability-weighted before implying expected regret.
